6	\paragraph{Confounds and transferability.}
7	Three observations argue against a topic shortcut: unstable topic OOD (F1\,=\,0.541$\pm$0.207), scrambled labels within 3.9\,pp, and label-free MLM detection (F1\,$\geq$\,0.97). ToxicChat (+18--42\,pp; Table~\ref{tab:human_data}) and cross-LLM experiments (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_llm}) confirm transfer; mixed-LLM 9-class maintains DD F1\,=\,0.818$\pm$0.096 while MLM collapses to vanilla level (Appendix~\ref{app:mixed_llm}). Dataset composition---not sample count---is the primary determinant (Appendix~\ref{app:scale_validation}). Prompt optimization improves LLM-as-judge OOD detection but does not close the gap with encoder-based methods (Appendix~\ref{app:prompt_opt}).
8	
9	\paragraph{Evidence for discriminative compression.}
10	Classification DAPT compresses the feature space into few discriminative dimensions (participation ratio 3.7--9.4 vs.\ 25--32 for MLM/vanilla) with 3--5 orders of magnitude higher separability (Fisher 2.6--19.7 vs.\ $<$0.001; Appendix~\ref{app:representation}). Scrambled labels produce comparable compression (Fisher\,=\,2.6), consistent with \citet{zhang2021understanding}'s framework for how classification modifies representations, with two domain-specific findings: (i)~domain-relevant label partitioning is necessary (topic labels insufficient), and (ii)~compression localizes to 3 SVD dimensions in layers 8--12 (Fisher 15$\times$ vanilla at peak; Appendix~\ref{app:layerwise}), with early layers remaining label-independent as in \citet{maennel2020random}. A discriminative probe confirms: 3 SVD components of 9-class layer-10 representations achieve perfect AA OOD detection (F1\,=\,1.000), while random projections degrade to 0.928 and MLM reaches only 0.636 with all 768 dimensions (Appendix~\ref{app:discriminative_probe}). The complexity-dependent boundary persists (TOST $d < 0.1$ on D
